<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<schema xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:diggs="http://diggsml.org/schemas/3"
    xmlns:gml="http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2"
    xmlns:vc="http://www.w3.org/2007/XMLSchema-versioning" vc:maxVersion="1.1" vc:minVersion="1.0"
    elementFormDefault="qualified" targetNamespace="http://diggsml.org/schemas/3" version="3.1.0">

    <annotation>
        <documentation>Schema for DIGGS Specification Registries.

            A specification registry is a standalone, authority-controlled document holding one
            canonical diggs:Specification object per published standard - an ASTM or AASHTO method,
            a national or European standard, an agency special provision - so that a data provider
            cites a standard by reference rather than retyping its bibliographic details into every
            instance document. It is an authority artifact of the same kind as a code list
            dictionary, NOT project data: registries are published and versioned by DIGGS (or
            another named authority), never assembled inside a project's own instance document.

            WHY THIS EXISTS. diggs:Specification's own documentation (core/Common.xsd) states that
            it "exists to CITE a published standard - its properties are standardReferenceNumber,
            standardTitle, standardVersion and so on - and it states no requirement of its own."
            Until now there was nowhere to put a canonical one, so every provider inlined their own,
            and the same standard was described a dozen different ways. That variation is what a
            registry removes.

            HOW A CITATION IS VALIDATED. Every property typed diggs:SpecificationPropertyType
            carries gml:AssociationAttributeGroup, so an instance may write

            &lt;governingStandard xlink:href="https://diggsml.org/def/registry/DIGGS/0/rigidInclusions.xml#astm_d1143"/&gt;

            with no schema change of any kind. Validation of that reference has two layers, and both
            reuse machinery that already exists:

            1. TARGET CLASS - the citing element carries
            &lt;appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement"&gt;, the established DIGGS convention (46 uses
            across the schema; diggs:AbstractAcceptanceCriterionType/sourceSpecRef already names
            diggs:Specification this way). diggs:RegisteredSpecification substitutes
            diggs:Specification, so a reference into a registry satisfies that declaration by
            substitution - exactly as references to abstract heads such as
            diggs:AbstractProgramModule already do.

            2. TARGET APPLICABILITY - the question "is this citing element allowed to cite THIS
            standard?" is answered by the diggs:occurrences property on the registry entry itself,
            reusing diggs:OccurrenceArrayPropertyType from Dictionary_diggs.xsd unchanged. An entry
            declares the XPath(s) at which it may legitimately be cited, so a lab-sample preparation
            standard cannot be cited as a rigid inclusion design basis. This is the identical
            mechanism, in the identical shape, that dictionary-validation.xsl already applies to a
            code list Definition - the constraint travels with the registered term, so the
            validator stays generic and never needs per-domain knowledge or a lookup table.

            The parallel with Dictionary_diggs.xsd is deliberate and exact. There, diggs:Definition
            extends a GML base and adds authority, reference and occurrences; here,
            diggs:RegisteredSpecification extends diggs:SpecificationType and adds the same three
            plus registry lifecycle. A dictionary controls the VALUE of a coded property; a registry
            controls the IDENTITY of a referenced object. Same problem, same solution shape,
            different kind of target.

            SCOPE. One registry per domain, mirroring how def's dictionaries are already organized
            by domain (gp_, pil_, mwd_, cpt_, gr_inj_, ri_design_). Rigid inclusions is the first;
            laboratory testing, in situ testing, sampling and construction follow.</documentation>
    </annotation>

    <import namespace="http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2" schemaLocation="core/gml3.2Profile_diggs.xsd"/>

    <!-- ==================== Registry root ==================== -->

    <element name="SpecificationRegistry" type="diggs:SpecificationRegistryType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The root element of a specification registry document. Holds the
                registry's own identity - gml:identifier carrying the registry's canonical URL,
                gml:name, gml:description - inherited from gml:AbstractGMLType, followed by its
                entries.

                A registry document is validated against Diggs.xsd like any other DIGGS document;
                this element is its root, in the same way gml:Dictionary is the root of a code list
                document. It is NOT reachable from diggs:Diggs and must never be embedded in a
                project instance: a registry is published by an authority, and a project cites it.

                Scope one registry to one domain and say so in gml:description. A registry that
                tries to hold every standard in geotechnics becomes unreviewable, and the per-entry
                diggs:occurrences declarations are what actually enforce domain boundaries
                anyway.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>

    <complexType name="SpecificationRegistryType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the root of a specification registry. Extends
                gml:AbstractGMLType so that a registry carries gml:id, gml:identifier, gml:name and
                gml:description exactly as a gml:Dictionary does - the registry's own citable
                identity, distinct from the identity of the standards it holds.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="gml:AbstractGMLType">
                <sequence>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="registryEntry"
                        type="diggs:RegisteredSpecificationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>One entry of the registry, containing exactly one
                                diggs:RegisteredSpecification. Repeatable; a registry with no
                                entries is permitted so that a domain registry can be published
                                before it is populated.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>

    <complexType name="RegisteredSpecificationPropertyType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Property type containing one diggs:RegisteredSpecification.

                Deliberately carries NO gml:AssociationAttributeGroup. A registry OWNS its entries -
                they are held by value, never by reference - and permitting an xlink:href here would
                allow a registry to be assembled by pointing at entries held elsewhere, which
                defeats the point of a single authority-controlled document. Ownership is
                expressible, association is not.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <sequence>
            <element ref="diggs:RegisteredSpecification"/>
        </sequence>
        <attributeGroup ref="gml:OwnershipAttributeGroup"/>
    </complexType>

    <!-- ==================== Registry entry ==================== -->

    <element name="RegisteredSpecification" type="diggs:RegisteredSpecificationType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:Specification">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A diggs:Specification as published in a registry: the canonical citation
                of one standard, or of one named part, clause or procedure of a standard, plus the
                registry metadata that makes it citable and checkable.

                SUBSTITUTES diggs:Specification, which is what makes the whole design work with no
                change to any existing DIGGS content model. Every one of the fifteen properties
                typed diggs:SpecificationPropertyType therefore accepts a reference to a registry
                entry, and any consumer or validator that understands diggs:Specification
                understands this too. The bibliographic properties are inherited unchanged from
                diggs:SpecificationType; nothing about how a standard is described is altered by
                registering it.

                CHOOSING gml:id. The gml:id is the citation token - it is what appears after the '#'
                in every xlink:href - so it should track the standard's own identifier as closely as
                XML permits. gml:id is an XML ID and therefore an NCName: it may not begin with a
                digit and may not contain a space, a slash or a colon. So "ASTM D1143" becomes
                astm_d1143 and "BS 8006-1:2010" becomes bs_8006_1_2010. Use lower snake case
                throughout, and keep the token stable forever once published - a registry whose
                identifiers churn is worse than no registry, because existing citations silently
                break.

                SUBMETHODS. A named procedure within a standard is a separate entry, not a variant
                of one: ASTM D1143 Procedure A is its own diggs:RegisteredSpecification. Give it its
                own gml:id (astm_d1143_proc_a), repeat the parent's standardReferenceNumber, and
                distinguish it by standardClause, standardPart or shortMethodName. Because those are
                structured properties rather than free text, a consumer can group every entry
                sharing a standardReferenceNumber under its parent standard mechanically. That
                grouping is the only reason to prefer registering a submethod over inventing an
                ad hoc code for it.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>

    <complexType name="RegisteredSpecificationType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Extended type based on diggs:SpecificationType. Adds the registry
                metadata - controlling authority, source URL, lifecycle status and the occurrence
                declarations that bound where the entry may be cited.

                Every added property is optional, so a diggs:RegisteredSpecification is a valid
                diggs:Specification in every context the latter is allowed. That is intentional: it
                means an instance may inline a registry entry verbatim where a reference is
                impractical - an air-gapped exchange, an archival snapshot - without inventing a
                second representation of the same standard.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:SpecificationType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="authority" type="string">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Name of the organization that controls the registry entry
                                - normally DIGGS. Distinct from the inherited accreditingBody, which
                                names the body that publishes the STANDARD (ASTM International,
                                BSI, CEN). Mirrors diggs:DefinitionType/authority.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="reference" type="anyURI">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A URI for the standard itself - the publisher's landing
                                page or DOI - so a reader can reach the source document. Mirrors
                                diggs:DefinitionType/reference. This is where the standard LIVES,
                                not where the registry entry lives; the latter is the entry's own
                                gml:identifier.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="registryStatus"
                        type="diggs:RegistryStatusEnumType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Lifecycle state of the registered standard. A registry
                                must be able to say that a standard has been withdrawn or
                                superseded WITHOUT deleting its entry, because instances published
                                while it was current still cite it and those citations must keep
                                resolving. ASTM D1194 is the worked example: withdrawn, but present
                                in the historical record of plate load testing.

                                Absent means active; state it explicitly for anything that is
                                not.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="supersededByRef"
                        type="diggs:FeatureReferenceType">
                        <annotation>
                            <appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement">diggs:Specification</appinfo>
                            <documentation>For an entry whose registryStatus is superseded or
                                withdrawn, a reference to the entry that replaces it - normally
                                another diggs:RegisteredSpecification in the same registry. Lets a
                                consumer follow a historical citation forward to the current
                                standard.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="occurrences"
                        type="diggs:OccurrenceArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>One or more diggs:Occurrence objects declaring where this
                                entry may legitimately be cited: sourceElementXpath gives the
                                element that may hold a reference to it, and the optional
                                conditionalElementXpath gives an element (and optionally a value)
                                that must be present for the citation to be valid.

                                THIS IS THE DOMAIN CHECK. Resolving an xlink:href proves only that
                                the target exists and is a diggs:Specification; it cannot tell a
                                rigid inclusion design basis from a laboratory sample preparation
                                method, since both are Specifications. These declarations are what
                                make the distinction machine-checkable, and they are read from the
                                target rather than configured into the validator - so the validator
                                stays generic and vocabulary-independent, exactly as
                                dictionary-validation.xsl already is for code lists.

                                An entry with no occurrences declares no restriction and may be
                                cited anywhere a diggs:Specification is permitted. Prefer stating
                                them: an unbounded entry is the registry equivalent of an
                                unconditioned dictionary Occurrence, and those have been a recurring
                                source of misuse.

                                Reuses diggs:OccurrenceArrayPropertyType from Dictionary_diggs.xsd
                                without modification. The shape is not dictionary-specific - it is a
                                general "this term is permitted here, conditionally on that" - and
                                reusing it rather than inventing a parallel form is what lets one
                                validator serve both mechanisms.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>

    <!-- ==================== Enumerations ==================== -->

    <simpleType name="RegistryStatusEnumType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Lifecycle states of a registered specification. Closed, because the three
                states are exhaustive: a standard is current, replaced by a named successor, or
                retired without one.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <restriction base="string">
            <enumeration value="active">
                <annotation>
                    <documentation>Current and in force. The default reading when registryStatus is
                        absent.</documentation>
                </annotation>
            </enumeration>
            <enumeration value="superseded">
                <annotation>
                    <documentation>Replaced by a later standard, which should be named in
                        supersededByRef. The entry is retained so existing citations keep
                        resolving.</documentation>
                </annotation>
            </enumeration>
            <enumeration value="withdrawn">
                <annotation>
                    <documentation>Withdrawn by its publisher without a direct replacement. Retained
                        for the same reason: instances published while it was current still cite
                        it.</documentation>
                </annotation>
            </enumeration>
        </restriction>
    </simpleType>

</schema>
